Subject: network: QNetworkAccessManager's HTTP stack without threads

WHAT / WHY

  src/network/configure.cmake
      `qt_feature("http" ... CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread)`. With
      FEATURE_thread=OFF -- which this port cannot change, because wasi-libc's
      pthread_create is a stub returning ENOTSUP -- that excludes the ENTIRE
      QT_FEATURE_http source scope from libQt6Network: qhttpnetworkconnection,
      all of http2/, qnetworkreplyhttpimpl, qrestaccessmanager,
      qhttpsocketengine. QNetworkAccessManager survives but can only do
      file:// and data://. Qt's own wasm build dodges this with
      qnetworkreplywasmimpl.cpp under `CONDITION WASM`, i.e. it hands the
      whole job to the browser's fetch(); we are not WASM and have no browser.
      The condition is dropped and the three thread assumptions behind it are
      fixed below.

  src/network/access/qnetworkaccessmanager.cpp
      createThread() does `new QThread; thread->start()`. With
      QT_CONFIG(thread) off that object is not a thread: qthread.cpp's
      non-thread QThreadPrivate gives it its OWN QThreadData with no event
      dispatcher, start() only sets a flag, and nothing ever drains its
      post-event queue -- so moving the delegate onto it makes every
      QueuedConnection to the delegate vanish silently. Returning
      QThread::currentThread() instead is not an approximation of threading,
      it is the CORRECT semantics for a single-threaded process:

        * moveToThread(currentThread()) short-circuits to "already in this
          thread" and returns true;
        * the delegate's QueuedConnections are delivered by the application's
          own event loop, in order;
        * the BlockingQueuedConnections become DIRECT calls -- qobject.cpp
          wraps that entire arm of QMetaObject::activate() in
          `#if QT_CONFIG(thread)` (qobject.cpp:4094 in 6.8.4), so with threads
          off it falls through to the direct path. Which is exactly what
          "block until the other thread has answered" degenerates to when
          there is no other thread.

      destroyThread() correspondingly must not run: quitting the caller's
      thread would exit the application's event loop.

  src/network/access/qnetworkreplyhttpimpl.cpp
      The same two changes for the internal SynchronousRequestAttribute path,
      which builds its own QThread rather than the manager's. Without them a
      synchronous reply would post startRequest to a dead queue and fail on
      QHttpThreadDelegate's own 30-second watchdog.

  src/network/access/qhttpthreaddelegate{_p.h,.cpp}
      `static QThreadStorage<QNetworkAccessCache *> connections;`.
      QThreadStorage's out-of-line half lives in qthreadstorage.cpp, which
      src/corelib/CMakeLists.txt compiles only under
      `CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread` -- so the template header compiles fine and
      the APPLICATION link fails on QThreadStorageData. With one thread,
      thread-local is global; replaced by a three-method struct over a plain
      static with the same hasLocalData/localData/setLocalData interface,
      including setLocalData's delete-the-previous-value behaviour that
      startRequestSynchronously() relies on.

  PROVEN, not argued: the wk-server test `qt_network_speaks_to_a_wk_node`
  drives a real QNetworkAccessManager::get() from a wk node against
  plugins/netserve on the same fabric and asserts HTTP STATUS 200 plus the
  body. HTTP/1.1 cleartext only -- QT_FEATURE_ssl is 0 on this platform, so
  https:// has no backend at all.

UPSTREAM
  not-as-is. The thread-storage and createThread hunks are honest
  single-thread fallbacks that upstream might take as `#if !QT_CONFIG(thread)`
  arms. Relaxing the feature CONDITION is a policy change they would want
  behind a real no-thread CI configuration first, and would probably want to
  do together with the QNetworkAccessManager synchronous path, HTTP/2 and the
  authentication cache getting the same review.

--- a/src/network/configure.cmake
+++ b/src/network/configure.cmake
@@ -261,7 +261,6 @@
     SECTION "Networking"
     LABEL "HTTP"
     PURPOSE "Provides support for the Hypertext Transfer Protocol in QNetworkAccessManager."
-    CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread
 )
 qt_feature_definition("http" "QT_NO_HTTP" NEGATE VALUE "1")
 qt_feature("udpsocket" PUBLIC
--- a/src/network/access/qnetworkaccessmanager.cpp
+++ b/src/network/access/qnetworkaccessmanager.cpp
@@ -1721,6 +1721,20 @@
 
 QThread * QNetworkAccessManagerPrivate::createThread()
 {
+#if !QT_CONFIG(thread)
+    // No threads on this platform (wasm32-wasip2). A QThread built with
+    // QT_CONFIG(thread) off is an object with its own QThreadData and no
+    // event dispatcher: start() only sets a flag, nothing ever drains its
+    // post-event queue, and moving the delegate onto it would silently
+    // swallow every QueuedConnection. Hand back the calling thread instead --
+    // then moveToThread() is a no-op, the delegate's queued signals go through
+    // the application's own event loop, and the BlockingQueuedConnections
+    // become direct calls (qobject.cpp compiles that arm out entirely when
+    // QT_CONFIG(thread) is 0, so activate() falls through to the direct
+    // path). That is the correct semantics for a single-threaded process, not
+    // an approximation of it.
+    return QThread::currentThread();
+#else
     if (!thread) {
         thread = new QThread;
         thread->setObjectName(QStringLiteral("QNetworkAccessManager thread"));
@@ -1728,10 +1742,16 @@
     }
     Q_ASSERT(thread);
     return thread;
+#endif
 }
 
 void QNetworkAccessManagerPrivate::destroyThread()
 {
+#if !QT_CONFIG(thread)
+    // createThread() never created one, and quitting the caller's thread here
+    // would exit the application's event loop.
+    Q_ASSERT(!thread);
+#else
     if (thread) {
         thread->quit();
         thread->wait(QDeadlineTimer(5000));
@@ -1741,6 +1761,7 @@
             QObject::connect(thread, SIGNAL(finished()), thread, SLOT(deleteLater()));
         thread = nullptr;
     }
+#endif
 }
 
 
--- a/src/network/access/qnetworkreplyhttpimpl.cpp
+++ b/src/network/access/qnetworkreplyhttpimpl.cpp
@@ -629,11 +629,20 @@
 
     QThread *thread = nullptr;
     if (synchronous) {
+#if !QT_CONFIG(thread)
+        // See QNetworkAccessManagerPrivate::createThread(). A synchronous
+        // request still works without threads: startRequestSynchronously()
+        // runs a nested QEventLoop, and because the delegate stays on this
+        // thread the QueuedConnection that starts the request is delivered by
+        // that nested loop rather than by a worker's.
+        thread = QThread::currentThread();
+#else
         // A synchronous HTTP request uses its own thread
         thread = new QThread();
         thread->setObjectName(QStringLiteral("Qt HTTP synchronous thread"));
         QObject::connect(thread, SIGNAL(finished()), thread, SLOT(deleteLater()));
         thread->start();
+#endif
     } else {
         // We use the manager-global thread.
         // At some point we could switch to having multiple threads if it makes sense.
@@ -997,12 +1006,14 @@
         if (delegate->incomingErrorCode != QNetworkReply::NoError)
             httpError(delegate->incomingErrorCode, delegate->incomingErrorDetail);
 
+#if QT_CONFIG(thread)
         thread->quit();
         thread->wait(QDeadlineTimer(5000));
         if (thread->isFinished())
             delete thread;
         else
             QObject::connect(thread, SIGNAL(finished()), thread, SLOT(deleteLater()));
+#endif
 
         finished();
     } else {
